"The Gambling Commission has published research suggesting that illegal gambling in Great Britain supplements licensed play rather than replacing it. Reported on 29 October 2026, the study used the Commission's Open Banking dataset to identify 3,426 transactions with suspected illegal operators, linked to 179 individuals. Of the people with repeated detected illegal transactions, 91 per cent also used licensed operators. For a sector that has long argued that excessive regulation pushes customers to the black market, the finding is uncomfortable.",
"The central point is the absence of displacement. Licensed gambling transactions did not fall substantially after consumers first used illegal sites. If customers were leaving the regulated market for unlicensed brands, a fall would be expected. Instead the data suggests that the same people use both. That affects how regulators and operators should discuss channelisation, because a policy that is judged only by whether customers move to illegal sites may overlook the question of how much additional harm they experience when they do.",
"The harm indicators are striking, though the sample is small. Of the 146 people with Problem Gambling Severity Index scores, 45 per cent scored 8 or higher, the threshold for problem gambling, compared with 6 per cent in the comparison group of consumers with licensed transactions. Credit cards funded 138 transactions, or 4 per cent, despite the ban on gambling with credit in Great Britain, while current accounts funded the remainder. The Commission also noted that illegal-site transactions tended to cluster in short periods, as did combined use of licensed and unlicensed operators.",
"The Commission was careful about the limits of the work. The dataset is not nationally representative and cannot give a definitive picture of the illegal market, and some transactions with unlicensed operators may have gone undetected. The researchers described it as a proof of concept for using financial transaction data to study illegal gambling. Legal advisers should therefore avoid overstating the numbers in client advice or in submissions, while recognising that the method itself is likely to be used again with larger datasets.",
"For licensed operators, the implications are practical. Customer risk assessments may need to consider the possibility that a customer who shows signs of harm is also gambling elsewhere, and that spending patterns on licensed accounts do not tell the whole story. Affordability and customer interaction processes under the Licence Conditions and Codes of Practice will continue to be judged against what the operator knew or should have known. Evidence that a business has considered available information about its customers' wider activity, within the limits of data protection law, will be valuable if a case is later reviewed.",
"For payment providers and banks, the study supports the use of transaction data as an enforcement and research tool, and it signals that the Commission expects financial institutions to play a role in disrupting payments to illegal operators. Businesses should expect further engagement on transaction monitoring and merchant category coding. Counsel should also watch how the findings are used in the current debate over licence fees and the funding of enforcement work against illegal gambling, where the Commission has stressed the need for resources."
